When a car operates at its rated (maximum) power, the traction force \( F \) is given by \( F = P / v \), where \( P \) is constant and \( v \) is speed. For constant linear motion, the net force must be zero (traction equals resistance). However, because \( F \) is inversely proportional to \( v \), any small change in speed would cause a change in \( F \) that makes net force non-zero, leading to acceleration or deceleration. This inherent instability prevents the car from maintaining a constant speed, so constant linear motion is impossible. In contrast, uniformly accelerated linear motion (B) can be achieved if the net force remains constant, which is possible when the decrease in traction is exactly compensated by changes in resistance; decelerating linear motion (C) is possible when resistance exceeds traction; and constant-speed circular motion (D) is achievable with constant speed and appropriate centripetal force.

